From 930a14e6d52128ddfb92f1d7a87d76b6a4f77bfe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yury Klushin <digger@live.ru>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 21:03:16 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] rt11: split syscalls.c one-function-per-file

Mirrors an existing convention already used by other split newlib
ports (e.g. libc/sys/rdos): one function per translation unit, so
plain archive-level linking from libc.a can exclude what a given
program never references, without needing -ffunction-sections (which
this pdp11 backend doesn't support at all).

- rt11fs.h: shared struct rt11_file, constants, and declarations for
  the functions below.
- rt11tab.c: the open-file table (rt11_files[]) and fd-to-slot lookup.
- rt11emt.c: raw EMT 375/374 wrappers.
- rt11buf.c: the per-file 512-byte block-buffering layer
  (rt11_ensure_block and friends).
- open.c: _open plus everything private to it only -- RADIX-50
  packing, name parsing, .SERR, the two byte-52-to-errno mappers.
  This is the one that actually matters for size: a program that
  never calls open() itself (the common case -- buffered stdio only
  ever calls read/write/close/fstat/lseek on the console fds, never
  open) never pulls any of open.c's own code in from libc.a. Measured
  on real .sav builds: a puts()-only program dropped by 2408 bytes
  (roughly matching open.o's own .text+.data size) purely from this
  split, no other code changes.
- read.c/write.c/close.c/lseek.c/fstat.c/isatty.c/sbrk.c/kill.c/
  getpid.c/link.c/unlink.c/stat.c: one function each.
- rt11exit.c, not exit.c: that name is reserved by newlib's own
  always-built libc/stdlib/exit.c (the public exit(), which calls
  this file's _exit after running atexit handlers) -- a same-named
  archive member here would collide with it, confirmed against
  libc/sys/rdos which avoids its own exit.c for the same reason.

Also fixes two latent signature mismatches against this port's own
declarations in sys/_default_fcntl.h and sys/unistd.h, exposed only
once building this against newlib's own freshly-generated headers
(as opposed to an already-installed, out-of-sync sysroot, which is
what had been used to verify every previous change in this port):
_open is now variadic (int, ...), matching its own prototype; _sbrk
now returns void * and takes ptrdiff_t, not caddr_t/int.

Verified via a full from-scratch toolchain rebuild (not just a manual
libc.a patch): the whole example/test suite builds and, run in the
ukncbtl-debugger emulator, passes end to end.
